  "summary": "I reconciled Walter Kim's (pt-0005) outpatient medication list per RBC-TOC-02, using the St. Brigid discharge summary of 10/19 and its signed addendum, which takes precedence where they differ. One item is unresolved and is flagged to you below.\n\n**Changes made:**\n- **Furosemide:** increased from 20 mg to 40 mg daily. The old order was replaced with a 40 mg tablet order (mr-t0001).\n- **Spironolactone:** started at 25 mg daily (mr-t0003). The discharge summary said 12.5 mg, but the signed addendum corrected it to 25 mg.\n- **Potassium chloride:** stopped (mr-t4-kcl).\n- **Lisinopril 10 mg daily:** the held order was resumed. The hospital said to restart once post-discharge creatinine was below 1.5. The outside lab report from 10/21 shows creatinine 1.32, so that condition is met.\n- **Follow-up lab:** I ordered a basic metabolic panel for 10/26–10/27 (sr-t0005). The discharge instructions call for one 1 week after starting spironolactone. It will also check potassium and creatinine after the other changes.\n\n**No change:**\n- **Metoprolol succinate ER 50 mg:** \"Metoprolol XL 50 mg\" on the discharge list is the same drug and dose under another name.\n- **Atorvastatin and aspirin:** continued as listed.\n- **Omeprazole:** kept. Pantoprazole was only the hospital's formulary substitute, so I did not order it.\n\n**Flagged to you (comm-t0006):** His home metoprolol tartrate 25 mg twice daily (for palpitations) is not mentioned in the discharge documents, so I left it unchanged. He is also on metoprolol succinate ER, which may mean he is taking two beta-blockers. Please decide whether the tartrate should continue.\n\nI documented all of this in a progress note (doc-t0007).",
